</div>

<section class="im-section im-wrap">
	<div class="im-prose">
		<h2>The hero, seven ways</h2>
		<p>One set of parts — breadcrumb, tags, title, excerpt, byline, picture — and seven arrangements. Nothing is added or taken away between them: <code>cover</code> and <code>minimal</code> hide what they do not use, in CSS. That is what lets a theme offer the choice as a <strong>setting</strong> instead of seven templates.</p>
		<p>The buttons above are that setting, and they set the columns at the same time, because a post layout is one decision and not two. Each press prints the two class lists it applied under the controls — copy them.</p>
		<div class="im-table-scroll">
		<table>
			<thead><tr><th>Class</th><th></th></tr></thead>
			<tbody>
				<tr><td><code>im-posthero-wide</code></td><td>Left-aligned, picture underneath. The default here, and what most publications want.</td></tr>
				<tr><td><code>im-posthero-stack</code></td><td>The same, centred.</td></tr>
				<tr><td><code>im-posthero-split</code></td><td>Copy left, picture right. Two columns from <code>lg</code>; below that it stacks and the picture lies down again.</td></tr>
				<tr><td><code>im-posthero-side</code></td><td>The mirror: picture left, copy right. One <code>order</code>, no second markup.</td></tr>
				<tr><td><code>im-posthero-cover</code></td><td>The picture is the background, the copy sits over it on a scrim. Add <code>im-posthero-cover-center</code> to put the title in the middle of the frame.</td></tr>
				<tr><td><code>im-posthero-minimal</code></td><td>No picture at all, a rule underneath. What an essay wants.</td></tr>
			</tbody>
		</table>
		</div>
		{{{{code "hbs"}}}}
{{#post}}{{> "components/post-hero" style=@custom.post_hero_style crumbs=true zoom=true section="Journal" section_url="/blog/"}}{{/post}}
		{{{{/code}}}}

		<h3>The trail, and who wrote it</h3>
		<p>Two small things carry more than they look like they should.</p>
		<ul>
			<li><strong>The breadcrumb has icons</strong> — a house, the collection, the topic — and the last crumb is this post, truncated rather than wrapped. It scrolls sideways on a phone instead of becoming three lines. <code>crumbs=true</code>; name the collection with <code>section</code> and <code>section_url</code>. See <a href="/navigation/breadcrumb/">Breadcrumb</a>.</li>
			<li><strong>The byline is a hover card.</strong> A name and an avatar under a headline tell a reader nothing; hover or focus them and the whole answer arrives — bio, where, how many posts, where else to find them — without a line of it being printed under every title. On a touch device the link simply goes to the author's page. See <a href="/components/tooltip/">Tooltip &amp; hover card</a>.</li>
			<li><strong>The feature image opens.</strong> <code>zoom=true</code> puts a frame mark in its corner and the picture on the sheet. The link <em>covers</em> the picture rather than wrapping it, because the two-column arrangements reach for <code>.im-posthero-media &gt; img</code> and a wrapper would break them.</li>
		</ul>

		<h3>Two widths hold it together</h3>
		<div class="im-table-scroll">
		<table>
			<thead><tr><th>Variable</th><th></th></tr></thead>
			<tbody>
				<tr><td><code>--im-posthero-measure</code></td><td>Caps the <strong>copy</strong> at 46rem — a title is never thrown across a 90rem window.</td></tr>
				<tr><td><code>--im-posthero-frame</code></td><td>Caps the <strong>picture</strong> at 56rem. A feature image allowed to fill the page is a wall, not a header. <code>none</code> in the two-column arrangements, where the picture is a column and the grid decides.</td></tr>
				<tr><td><code>--im-posthero-media</code></td><td>Its aspect ratio, <code>16 / 9</code>. In <code>split</code> and <code>side</code> there is no ratio at all: the picture is exactly as tall as the copy beside it, so the two columns end level whatever the headline runs to.</td></tr>
				<tr><td><code>--im-posthero-min</code></td><td>The floor for that, 24rem — so a two-line title with no excerpt does not produce a letterbox.</td></tr>
				<tr><td><code>--im-posthero-gap</code></td><td>Between the copy and the picture. Fluid.</td></tr>
			</tbody>
		</table>
		</div>

		<h2>The columns</h2>
		<p>Two layouts cover every post page, and both place their children by <strong>named grid areas</strong> — so a column moves, or goes, without the markup changing.</p>
		<div class="im-table-scroll">
		<table>
			<thead><tr><th>Class</th><th></th></tr></thead>
			<tbody>
				<tr><td><code>im-with-rails</code></td><td><strong>Three columns:</strong> a contents rail, the article, a column of widgets. For a long post on a wide screen, where the way in and the further reading are both worth having and neither should be stacked on the other. Children are <code>&lt;nav&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;article&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;aside&gt;</code>.</td></tr>
				<tr><td><code>im-with-aside im-with-aside-wide</code></td><td><strong>Two columns:</strong> the article and a 19rem column of widgets on the right — wide enough for an author card, a top five, a newsletter.</td></tr>
				<tr><td>…&nbsp;<code>im-with-aside-start</code></td><td>The same, on the <strong>left</strong>. Only the tracks swap.</td></tr>
				<tr><td><code>im-with-aside</code></td><td>The slim 13.5rem rail a table of contents wants, rather than a column of widgets.</td></tr>
				<tr><td>—</td><td>No wrapper at all: <code>im-wrap</code> and the article. Full width, the standard measure.</td></tr>
			</tbody>
		</table>
		</div>
		<p>Three columns become two under <code>xl</code> — the contents rail moves above the article and the widgets keep their column — and one under <code>lg</code>, in reading order: contents, article, widgets. In the two-column layout a <strong>contents</strong> rail moves above the article, because it is a way in, and a <strong>wide</strong> column of widgets stays below it, because it is further reading.</p>

		<h3>Both rails keep the same distance</h3>
		<p><code>im-sticky-rail</code> is what makes either column follow the reader, and <strong>one</strong> variable decides everything about how it sits:</p>
		<div class="im-table-scroll">
		<table>
			<thead><tr><th>Variable</th><th></th></tr></thead>
			<tbody>
				<tr><td><code>--im-rail-inset</code></td><td>1.25rem. The distance under the top bar — <em>and</em> the gap the rail keeps above the bottom of the window. One value, so a contents rail on the left and widgets on the right sit at exactly the same height and breathe exactly the same amount, whatever is inside them.</td></tr>
				<tr><td><code>--im-rail-gap</code></td><td>On <code>im-with-rails</code>: the gutter, used on <strong>both</strong> sides. The article sits the same distance from the contents as from the widgets. Two values here is how a three-column page starts looking lopsided.</td></tr>
			</tbody>
		</table>
		</div>
		<p>A rail is also <code>overflow-y: auto</code> and capped at the window height, so a long contents list or a tall stack of widgets scrolls on its own rather than pushing the page down.</p>

		<h3>Contents on the left means the series on the right</h3>
		<p>When there <em>is</em> a contents rail, the widget column should open with the <strong>series</strong>, not with the author. The two rails then answer the same question at two scales: the contents say where you are in this post, the series says where you are in the run of them. Both are navigation; the author, the popular list and the newsletter are not, and they belong under that.</p>
		<p>The series is the <a href="/components/timeline/">timeline</a> component in a widget — <code>im-timeline im-timeline-compact im-widget-series</code> — with <code>data-state</code> on each part: <code>done</code>, <code>current</code>, <code>upcoming</code>. The part you are reading is not a link to itself.</p>
		{{{{code "hbs"}}}}
<section class="im-widget">
	<h2 class="im-widget-title">{{t "This series"}} <a href="{{series.url}}">{{series.parts}} parts</a></h2>
	<ol class="im-timeline im-timeline-compact im-widget-series">
		{{#foreach series.posts}}
		<li class="im-timeline-item" data-state="{{#if current}}current{{else}}{{#if read}}done{{else}}upcoming{{/if}}{{/if}}">
			<span class="im-timeline-marker">{{number}}</span>
			<h3 class="im-timeline-title">{{#if current}}<span>{{title}}</span>{{else}}<a href="{{url}}">{{title}}</a>{{/if}}</h3>
		</li>
		{{/foreach}}
	</ol>
</section>
		{{{{/code}}}}

		<h2>After the last paragraph</h2>
		<p>A post that ends at its last sentence is a dead end. <code>im-postfoot</code> is the rhythm of everything that comes after it — one rule at the top, one gap between blocks, one caption style — and every block inside it is an ordinary component. A theme prints the ones it has, in the order it wants.</p>
		<div class="im-table-scroll">
		<table>
			<thead><tr><th>Block</th><th>Built from</th></tr></thead>
			<tbody>
				<tr><td>Tags &amp; share</td><td><code>im-postfoot-bar</code> — a cluster of <a href="/components/badge/#tag">tags</a> and the <a href="/components/share/">share</a> row. Always printed, if the post has tags.</td></tr>
				<tr><td>The author</td><td><code>im-author-card</code> — the whole answer the hover card only hinted at: avatar, name, where, bio, and where else to find them.</td></tr>
				<tr><td>Support</td><td><code>im-support</code> — buy a coffee, send a tip, become a member. <code>-fill</code> or <code>-accent</code> for the one block that is asking for something.</td></tr>
				<tr><td>More like this</td><td><code>im-related</code> — a grid of <a href="/collections/post/card/">post cards</a>, at <code>--im-related-min</code> each.</td></tr>
				<tr><td>Newsletter</td><td><code>im-postcta</code> + the <a href="/forms/inputs/">subscribe form</a>.</td></tr>
				<tr><td>Previous / next</td><td><code>im-postnav</code>, from <a href="/navigation/pagination/">Pagination</a> — two cards, sharing an edge, from Ghost's own <code>prev_post</code> and <code>next_post</code>.</td></tr>
				<tr><td>Comments</td><td><code>im-comments</code> — the thread, the box to write in, and a gate for members-only.</td></tr>
			</tbody>
		</table>
		</div>
		{{{{code "hbs"}}}}
{{#post}}
	{{#get "posts" filter="tags:[{{primary_tag.slug}}]+id:-{{id}}" limit="3" as |related|}}
		{{> "components/post-footer" author=true support=true newsletter=true nav=true
			related=related related_url="/blog/"}}
	{{/get}}
{{/post}}
		{{{{/code}}}}
		<p><strong>Comments.</strong> Ghost's own are one helper, <code>&#123;&#123;comments&#125;&#125;</code>, and they arrive in an iframe Ghost styles itself — nothing in this system reaches inside it. <code>im-comments</code> is for a theme printing its own thread: a head with the count, the box, a list, and replies nested <strong>once</strong>. A thread that indents forever is unreadable on a phone long before it is deep.</p>

		<h2>The whole of post.hbs</h2>
		{{{{code "hbs"}}}}
{{#post}}
	{{> "components/post-hero" style=@custom.post_hero_style crumbs=true zoom=true}}

	<div class="im-with-rails">
		<nav aria-label="On this page">
			<div class="im-sticky-rail"><div class="im-toc" data-toc="#article"></div></div>
		</nav>
		<article id="article">
			<section class="im-content im-content-lede" data-im-zoom>{{content}}</section>
			{{> "components/post-footer" author=true newsletter=true nav=true}}
		</article>
		<aside>
			<div class="im-sticky-rail"><div class="im-widgets">{{> "sidebar"}}</div></div>
		</aside>
	</div>
{{/post}}
		{{{{/code}}}}
		<p>A hero, the body, the blocks after it, and the columns around them. A theme renders only the columns it wants — there is no "hide the sidebar" class, because a column a theme does not want is a column it does not print. The controls at the top of this page keep all three in the markup and hide one with docs-only CSS, which is a demo's problem, not a theme's.</p>

		<h2>Details that matter</h2>
		<ul>
			<li><strong>Every picture carries the same mark.</strong> A frame in the top-right corner means "this opens" — on a gallery tile, on a carousel slide, on the feature image, on a picture in the body. One thing to learn, not four. <code>data-im-zoom</code> on the body does the last of those. See <a href="/media/gallery/">Gallery &amp; lightbox</a>.</li>
			<li><strong>The dots in the byline are drawn</strong>, not typed, so a screen reader does not read "bullet" between the date and the reading time.</li>
			<li><strong>The cover's scrim is a gradient, not a flat dim</strong> — dark where the words are, nearly clear at the top, so the photograph survives.</li>
			<li><strong>The title is <code>text-wrap: balance</code></strong> and the excerpt <code>pretty</code>: no one-word last line on either.</li>
			<li><strong>Two columns need room, not a breakpoint guess.</strong> <code>split</code> and <code>side</code> stack under <code>lg</code>, which is where a 4:5 picture beside 46rem of copy stops working.</li>
			<li><strong>The body is <code>im-content</code>, not <code>im-prose</code></strong> — a grid with three named widths, so a card the author set to <em>wide</em> or <em>full</em> in the editor breaks out of the text column with no negative margins. See <a href="/content/text/">Text &amp; lists</a>.</li>
		</ul>
